Move the degree vector to host memory before calling .numpy() on it.

run.py sets `device = args.gpu_id` -- an int -- and does
`dgl_graph = dgl_graph.to(device)`, which puts the graph on cuda:0 for any
non-negative gpu_id. Line 76 then calls `.numpy()` on a tensor derived from
it, which torch refuses for a CUDA tensor:

    TypeError: can't convert cuda:0 device type tensor to numpy.
    Use Tensor.cpu() to copy the tensor to host memory first.

So AD-GCL as published cannot complete on a GPU, which is the only mode its
README documents (`--gpu_id 0` in all six commands). Adding `.cpu()` is the
fix torch's own error message prescribes.

Nothing about the computation changes: `degree` is the same vector, read on
the host instead of the device, and it is used only for the high/low-degree
breakdown printed at the end.

Generated with `git diff` against the tree SOURCE_REF pins.

